Publish artifacts and skills from the browser

Settings > Artifacts and Settings > Share Skills both rendered a greyed toggle
reading "Desktop only. Open Settings on the host device to change this setting."
On orca-server the host is a headless box behind a proxy with no UI at all, so
that instruction named a screen that does not exist, and the capability could not
be granted from anywhere. The operator sits in the browser; the browser could
only read.

Upstream keeps `artifactSharingEnabled` and `agentSkillSharingEnabled` out of
`SettingsUpdate` on purpose, so no `settings.update` caller can grant itself
publishing - and `settings.update` IS on `MOBILE_RPC_METHOD_ALLOWLIST`. That
property is worth keeping on our host too, so the grant travels on a new
`settings.updateSharingCapabilities` instead, which is deliberately off the
allowlist. The owner enables publishing on the server; a paired phone inherits
that decision rather than making it.

Publishing itself is phone-reachable now: `artifacts.*` and the skill publish
rail join the allowlist. AGENTS.md keeps host-mutating and credential-minting
methods off it, and neither applies - these share ONE document each and none
admits a new client to the runtime, which is what `mobile.createPairingOffer` is
kept off for. The skill INSTALL rail stays off: it writes skill folders, which
can hold scripts, onto the host.

`skills.share` refused every paired client with "Run the command from Orca on the
machine that stores the skills." That encoded "your skills are elsewhere", which
is true for a laptop paired to someone else's desktop and false here - a browser
has no skill homes at all, so the only selectors any client can offer are the
ones `skills.discover` just returned from this host. The control the threat model
actually names is target resolution (TM-04 in
`docs/reference/agent-skill-sharing-threat-model.md`), and that is the
`native-host` check, which is unchanged.

`window.api.orcaProfiles.authStatus` answered a hardcoded
`{configured: false, state: 'unconfigured'}`, so the Artifacts page reported
"Orca account sign-in is not configured on this machine yet" for a host where it
is configured. It now asks the host over a new `orcaProfiles.authStatus`; the
access and refresh tokens never leave the host process. Reading it needs
`getCurrentOrcaProfileAuthStatus`, whose import chain reaches `electron` twice,
so it arrives through a settable registry in the overlay - the shape
`usage/usage-store-registry.ts` already uses - keeping
`config/runtime-electron-baseline.txt` empty.

Signing in still cannot be STARTED from the tile, and `connectCurrent` now says
so instead of blaming the host: upstream opens a loopback HTTP server on the
host's own 127.0.0.1 and hands it the authorization code, and login.onorca.dev
accepts loopback redirect URIs only - a proxy-origin callback is rejected with a
flat 400 and there is no device-code grant. The host also has no `xdg-open` or
browser binary for `shell.openExternal`. When a host session does exist it is
memory-only, because `safeStorage.isEncryptionAvailable()` is false with no
keyring and a packaged build refuses the plaintext fallback.

Five `window.api.skills` members that had a matching RPC now use it - preview,
remove, cancel and list installs. The publish and cloud-management members exist
only as `skills:*` ipcMain channels upstream and keep their current behaviour
rather than becoming new rejections; the tile's publish path is the toggle here
plus `skills.share`, which agents and the `orca` CLI on the host call.

Note for anyone editing `MOBILE_RPC_METHOD_ALLOWLIST`: no apostrophe may appear
inside that Set literal, comments included. `mobile-rpc-allowlist.test.ts`
extracts entries by scanning for single-quoted strings, so one stray quote
re-pairs every entry after it and drops them from the parsed set silently. A
contraction in a comment cost a green run here.

To test, open Settings > Artifacts in the tile with the host signed out: the
publish toggle should be usable, and turning it on should survive a reload and a
serve restart. Same in Settings > Share Skills, after which `orca skills share`
on the host stops answering `agent_skill_sharing_disabled`. Covered by
`sharing-surfaces.test.ts` for the two new methods and the strict grant schema,
`web-share-mobile-scope.test.ts` for what a paired phone may and may not reach,
`skills-paired-client-share.test.ts` for the lifted refusal and the checks that
stay, `web-share-surfaces.test.ts` for the preload routing, and
`share-surface-panes-web.test.tsx` for the two panes. All five are overlay files,
so popping this patch fails them rather than deleting them.
